# Regenerable caches. Everything here is rebuilt from configs/ by re-running a
# stage, so none of it belongs in git.
data/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- run outputs
#
# `runs/` is NOT regenerable, and treating it as though it were is the mistake
# these rules exist to fix. A run directory holds two very different kinds of
# thing:
#
#   * derived views -- reports that any laptop recomputes in seconds from the
#     artefacts below;
#   * measurements -- what the GPU session actually retrieved and generated,
#     what the judge actually scored, and what a human actually hand-scored.
#     Those cost GPU hours, API spend, or an afternoon of reading, and no
#     amount of re-running reproduces them locally.
#
# The same argument as the corpus manifest: the artefact that cannot be
# regenerated must not sit in a directory declared regenerable. So the default
# here is to ignore, and the measurements are named back in explicitly -- by
# what they ARE, never by run id, because the run id moves whenever the config
# does and a rule keyed to one would need editing every time.
runs/**
!runs/*/

# What the GPU produced.
!runs/*/retrieve/
!runs/*/retrieve/**
!runs/*/generate/
!runs/*/generate/**

# What the judge produced. API spend, and rate-limited.
!runs/*/judge/
!runs/*/judge/**

# Hand-scored calibration -- the least regenerable artefact in the project,
# since re-running the sampler reproduces the questions but not the judgement
# that an answer was wrong. The blind key sits beside them so the agreement
# figures can be recomputed.
!runs/*/calibration_*

# The aggregated reports. Cheap to recompute, but they are what gets read and
# quoted, and a report committed beside its inputs is a record of what the
# numbers were when they were written.
!runs/*/*_report.json

# The thesis figures. Regenerable like the reports, and tracked for the same
# reason with more force: these are the objects that end up in the document, so
# what the figure showed when it was written belongs in the history beside the
# numbers it drew.
!runs/*/figures/
!runs/*/figures/**

# What the run was configured with. Small, and it is what makes a run
# self-describing: the directory name can be re-derived from this file, so a
# result can be checked against the config that produced it rather than trusted
# because of where it sits.
!runs/*/resolved_config.json

# Archives made for moving results off Kaggle. Their contents are tracked
# above; the zip is a transport detail and a binary blob.
runs/**/*.zip
